<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-2-4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Functions as Mathematical Models</title>

<assemblage component="instructor">
<p>In this lesson students are asked to match the verbal description of a model with an appropriate formula and one of the basic graphs. We discuss the shape of a graph, particularly its concavity, as it applies to modeling.</p> 

<p>GOALS</p>
<p><ul>
    <li><p>Write a function for a situation described in words</p></li> 
    <li><p>Choose one of the basic graphs to fit a situation </p></li>   
    <li><p>Find a constant of proportionality</p></li>    
    <li><p>Recognize the graph of a basic function </p></li>
</ul></p></assemblage>

<activity xml:id="act-2-4-1"><title>Modeling with the Basic Functions</title>

          <aside component="instructor">
          <p>Begin the class with a brief discussion of concavity and changing slopes. The graphs in this Activity show only the first quadrant, so students must look more closely at their shapes to choose the appropriate graph for each formula. While reading the descriptions, they will also be reviewing the language of proportionality.</p></aside>

<introduction><p>Here are some situations that can be modeled by variations of the basic functions. Match each situation with a function from the list below, and with the appropriate graph. Write your function with the variables in the description, and answer any questions about the model.
        <md><mrow>y=kx^2 ~~~~~ \amp y=kx^3 ~~~~~ y=k\sqrt{x}</mrow>
            <mrow>y=k\sqrt[3]{x} ~~~~~ \amp y=\dfrac{k}{x} ~~~~~~~ y=\dfrac{k}{x^2}</mrow></md></p>
      <image source="act-5-6-1.png" width="90%"></image>
</introduction>

          <aside component="instructor">
          <p>Students may need help distinguishing between the graphs of <m>y=x^2</m> and <m>y=x^3</m>, between <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> and <m>y=\sqrt[3]{x}</m>, and between <m>y=\dfrac{1}{x}</m> and <m>y=\dfrac{1}{x^2}</m>.</p></aside>

<p><ol>
<li><p>The time, <m>t</m>, it takes to travel a certain distance, <m>d</m>, is inversely proportional to the speed, <m>r</m>, at which you travel. For example, to travel 400 miles at an average speed of 50 miles per hour will take 8 hours.</p>
  <p><ol marker="a.">
    <li>Write a formula for <m>t</m> as a function of <m>r</m>.</li>
    <li>Choose the appropriate graph, and label the axes.</li>
    <li>If you double your average speed, what happens to the travel time?</li>
  </ol></p>

      <aside component="instructor">
          <p>For each model, students can answer the last question using either the formula or the graph, and can perhaps use one to verify the other.</p></aside></li>

<li><p>The distance, <m>D</m>, needed to stop a car is proportional to the square of its velocity, <m>v</m>. A car traveling at 50 miles per hour needs about 170 feet to stop.</p>
  <p><ol marker="a.">
    <li>Write a formula for <m>D</m> as a function of <m>v</m>.</li>
    <li>Choose the appropriate graph, and label the axes.</li>
    <li>What happens to the stopping distance if the velocity of the car is doubled?</li>
  </ol></p>

      <aside component="instructor">
          <p>The first model used the familiar formula <m>d=rt</m> (or <m>t=\dfrac{d}{r}</m>), but in the other models you may need to remind students how to find the constant <m>k</m>, from the values given.</p></aside></li>

<li><p>The intensity, <m>I</m>, of sound is proportional to the reciprocal of the square of the distance, <m>d</m>, from its source. In a certain auditorium, the sound intensity 6 meters from the stage is 100 watts per square meter.</p>
  <p><ol marker="a.">
    <li>Write a formula for <m>I</m> as a function of <m>d</m>.</li>
    <li>Choose the appropriate graph, and label the axes.</li>
<li>What happens to the sound intensity if the distance from the stage is doubled?</li>
  </ol></p></li>

<li><p>The amount of energy, <m>E</m>, generated by a windmill is proportional to the cube of the wind speed, <m>v</m>. When the wind speed is 8 meters per second, the windmill generates 314 watts per square meter of energy.</p>
  <p><ol marker="a.">
    <li>Write a formula for <m>E</m> as a function of <m>v</m>.</li>
    <li>Choose the appropriate graph, and label the axes.</li>
    <li>What happens to the energy generated if the wind speed is doubled?</li>
  </ol></p></li>

<li><p>The speed, <m>S</m>, of a tsunami is proportional to the square root of the depth, <m>D</m>, of the ocean at that point. At a depth of 25 meters, a tsunami travels at 15.65 meters per second.</p>
  <p><ol marker="a.">
    <li>Write a formula for <m>S</m> as a function of <m>D</m>.</li>
    <li>Choose the appropriate graph, and label the axes.</li>
    <li>What happens to the speed of the tsunami if the depth of the ocean is doubled?</li>
  </ol></p></li>

<li><p>The height, <m>h</m>, of water in a conical storage tank is proportional to the cube root of the volume, <m>V</m>, of the water. If you pour 8 cubic feet of water into the tank, the water will reach a height of 3 feet.</p>
  <p><ol marker="a.">
  <li>Write a formula for <m>h</m> as a function of <m>V</m>.</li>
  <li>Choose the appropriate graph, and label the axes.</li>
  <li>What happens to the height of the water if you double the volume of water you pour in?</li>
  </ol></p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Explain how to find a constant of proportionality.</p></li>
    <li><p>How did you tell the difference between the graph of <m>y=x^2</m> and <m>y=x^3</m>?</p></li>
    <li><p>How did you tell the difference between the graph of <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> and <m>y=\sqrt[3]{x}</m>?</p></li>
    <li><p>How did you tell the difference between the graph of <m>y=\dfrac{1}{x}</m> and <m>y=\dfrac{1}{x^2}</m>?</p></li>
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
  <li><p>Describe and compare the graphs of the eight basic functions, including intervals where the graphs are positive or negative, increasing or decreasing, and concave up or down.</p></li>
  <li><p>Explain the difference between an empirical model and a mechanistic model.</p></li>
  <li><p>How can you tell from a table of values whether a function is concave up or concave down?</p></li>
  <li><p>What does an open circle on the graph of a piecewise function mean?</p></li>
</ol></p>
</assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>A graph whose slopes decrease for increasing <m>x</m> is called</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
            <li>Increasing</li>
            <li>Decreasing</li>
            <li>Concave up</li>
            <li>Concave down</li>
        </ol></p></li>
  <li><p>Which graph is concave down wherever it is defined?</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
            <li><m>y=x^3</m></li>
            <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{x}</m></li>
            <li><m>y=\sqrt[3]{x}</m></li>
            <li><m>y=\sqrt{x}</m></li>
        </ol></p></li>  
  <li><p>To decide which basic function might model a set of data, we can</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
            <li>plot the data.</li>
            <li>look at the variables.</li>
            <li>choose scales for the axes.</li>
            <li>calculate the slope.</li>
        </ol></p></li>
  <li><p>A piecewise function</p>
      <p><ol marker="a.">
            <li>must have no breaks in its graph.</li>
            <li>can have more than one output at some inputs.</li>
            <li>cannot have concavity.</li>
            <li>none of the above.</li>
          </ol></p></li>
  </ol></p></assemblage>


</section>
